{
  "original_id": "primary-20260914-vishnyakova-shifts",
  "archive_id": "BW2-PR20260914-vishnyakova_shifts",
  "title": "Interval shape and endpoint collisions for totally positive shifted even powers",
  "batch_label": "primary",
  "kind": "result-note",
  "original_status": "unresolved",
  "scope": "For every even integer n>=2, for the sets M_n defined verbatim in source.md and precisely in problem.md, there exists c_n with M_n=[-c_n,1+c_n] and (sqrt(2)-1)/2<=c_n<1-1/sqrt(2). At t=-c_n, Q_(n,t) has exactly one double zero r in (-1,0), all other zeros are simple and smaller than r, partial_z^2 Q_(n,t)(r)>0, and partial_t Q_(n,t)(r)<0; the other endpoint has the reflected configuration. The proof establishes the following operator theorem: if -3/10<=t<0, h has even degree d>=2, positive leading coefficient, and d simple zeros in (-1,0), S=h'(0)/h(0), delta=(1+2t)/(1+t)^2, and 0<lambda<=1/(S+delta), then T_t^2[(z-lambda)h] has d+2 simple zeros in (-1,0) and one simple positive zero, where T_t p=z(1+z)p'+(z+t)p. Equality in the lambda bound is included. Consequently every odd Q_(m,t), m>=1, has m-1 simple zeros in (-1,0) and one simple positive zero for -3/10<=t<0. The original successive-nesting assertion c_n<=c_(n+2) is not claimed and remains unresolved.",
  "gate_basis": "Current released terminal, manifest, PDF, TeX freeze, snapshot files, provenance and four audits agree.",
